<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is the maximum incline angle for a belt conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>It depends on the belt surface and the product. Dorner limits smooth flat belts to angles under 20 degrees and rates <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belts</a> up to 60 degrees. Forbo Siegling states certain patterned belts reach 30 degrees without profiles, and Hytrol's SBI rough-top incline adjusts up to 30 degrees. Sidewall belts with cross cleats convey bulk material up to 90 degrees. Test with your actual product, because weight, bottom surface, moisture, and dust all change the usable angle.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between an incline conveyor and a Z-frame conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A straight incline conveyor is a single angled run. A Z-frame conveyor adds horizontal sections at both ends (infeed and discharge) to form a Z-shape. The horizontal sections provide smooth product loading and unloading without noseover challenges. Z-frames use a single continuous belt through all three sections.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I prevent products from sliding on an incline conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Use rough-top or textured belt surfaces for moderate inclines. Add cleats (flights) perpendicular to belt travel for steeper angles. Use sidewalls (corrugated edges) to contain loose or bulk materials. Use vacuum hold-down for lightweight or flat products. For round or cylindrical products, use cleats or contoured guides to keep them from rolling. Always test with actual product samples.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">Can incline conveyors also run in decline mode?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Often, yes, if the drive is specified for it. Many incline conveyors can run as declines by reversing the belt or repositioning the unit. In decline service the load drives the belt downhill, so the drive needs braking capacity, such as a brake motor, a VFD with a braking resistor, or a regenerative drive, to hold belt speed. Without it, the belt overruns and products pile up at the discharge.</p>

</div>
</div>
